WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.388
One more thing changes, and users
will feel it: how they sign in.

2
00:00:04.388 --> 00:00:10.002
OMB M-22-09 requires phishing-resistant
multifactor authentication

3
00:00:10.002 --> 00:00:13.663
for agency staff, and it names
PIV as the simplest route.

4
00:00:13.663 --> 00:00:15.874
That reorders the usual roadmap.

5
00:00:15.874 --> 00:00:19.808
Earlier plans treated smart-card
sign-in as the last phase,

6
00:00:19.808 --> 00:00:21.775
for high-security users only.

7
00:00:21.775 --> 00:00:25.415
But a federal workforce already
carries PIV or CAC cards,

8
00:00:25.415 --> 00:00:30.681
so Entra certificate-based authentication
is the fastest path: the credential is

9
00:00:30.681 --> 00:00:36.025
already issued, and it works directly
against Entra ID without a federation server.

10
00:00:36.025 --> 00:00:37.725
The book runs four waves.

11
00:00:37.725 --> 00:00:42.750
Privileged accounts first, on FIDO2
security keys, or their PIV card.

12
00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:48.037
Then everyone who holds a PIV or CAC card,
through certificate-based authentication.

13
00:00:48.037 --> 00:00:52.126
Then managed Windows devices,
with Windows Hello for Business.

14
00:00:52.126 --> 00:00:55.383
And finally everyone else,
including mobile users,

15
00:00:55.383 --> 00:00:58.175
with passkeys in Microsoft Authenticator.

16
00:00:58.175 --> 00:01:02.513
All four meet Entra's built-in
phishing-resistant authentication strength.

17
00:01:02.513 --> 00:01:05.763
Some things go away: SMS
and voice codes for staff,

18
00:01:05.763 --> 00:01:10.232
which the memo requires agencies
to stop using for routine access,

19
00:01:10.232 --> 00:01:14.376
and legacy protocols that send
a password with every request.

20
00:01:14.376 --> 00:01:17.114
Those are found in the
sign-in logs, fixed,

21
00:01:17.114 --> 00:01:19.937
then blocked, and watched for thirty days.

22
00:01:19.937 --> 00:01:22.738
One thing stays on: password hash sync.

23
00:01:22.738 --> 00:01:25.519
It enables leaked-credential detection,

24
00:01:25.519 --> 00:01:29.830
and it's the manual fallback if
pass-through agents or AD FS fail.
